      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 491,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant, essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Formula ($H_2O$):** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**. \n* **States of Matter:** Water is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n  * **Liquid** (the water we drink, rivers, oceans)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity)\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water’s chemical structure (which has a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a slight negative charge on the oxygen side) allows it to dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can easily carry minerals, nutrients, and chemicals.\n\n### 2. The Ecological and Geological View\n* **Earth's Covering:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in the oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Water is constantly moving and recycling. It evaporates from oceans and lakes into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to the earth as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back into bodies of water. The amount of water on Earth is essentially constant and has been for billions of years.\n\n### 3. The Biological View (Life-Giver)\n* **Essential for Life:** As far as science knows, life cannot exist without water. \n* **Inside Living Things:** \n  * The human body is made up of about **60% water**.\n  * Water acts as a lubricant for joints, regulates body temperature (through sweating), and flushes out waste.\n  * At a cellular level, water provides the watery environment (cytoplasm) in which all the metabolic chemical reactions of life take place.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. Without it, weather wouldn't exist, ecosystems would collapse, and life as we know it would instantly cease to exist."
